WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.920
Is a potentially-repeatable recursion source&nbsp;
like Evolution Witness a card draw spell? How&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:10.160
about something that draws and discards like&nbsp;
Matzalantli, the Great Door, is that considered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:15.920
a draw spell? What about so-called impulse&nbsp;
draw options like Laelia, the Blade Reforged?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:21.520
I imagine you might have opinions on any one&nbsp;
of these questions. Maybe you said yes to all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:27.440
maybe you said yes to none, and maybe you’re&nbsp;
a diehard Great Door fan, no more no less.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:32.960
It would be satisfying and lovely if I could just&nbsp;
flat out say exactly what does and does not count&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:39.040
as card draw among these, but unfortunately it’s&nbsp;
a bit more complicated than that. Fundamentally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:43.920
the source of the ambiguity here is that card&nbsp;
draw as a concept often gets treated as one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:48.720
singular thing, but it’s actually more like a&nbsp;
web of overlapping solutions to a handful of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:54.560
different goals, and different decks will want the&nbsp;
sorts of things deemed “card draw” for completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.560 --> 00:01:00.000
different reasons. The goals of card draw are a&nbsp;
little fuzzy, but broadly there are two main ones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:06.320
that most decks will be focused on: spending&nbsp;
excess mana, and finding particular cards.

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:10.800
Let’s start with the first one. That word&nbsp;
“excess” actually might be a bit misleading,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:14.480
because for the sorts of decks that will most&nbsp;
want to think about their card draw this way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:19.040
getting excess mana is often a large point&nbsp;
of the deck. The most obvious deck style&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:22.880
that fits within this description would be&nbsp;
battlecruiser midrange, a type of deck that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:28.000
is typically lategame-focused and involves lots&nbsp;
of ramp. My Radha deck is an example of this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:32.560
and it’s a good style of deck to build for players&nbsp;
newer to the commander format because it follows a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:37.680
simple principle—spending more mana than opponents&nbsp;
will generally make it easier to defeat them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:42.240
regardless of what else is happening. In&nbsp;
this framework, this requires two simple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:47.360
steps—obtain a larger total pool of resources&nbsp;
through ramp, and then figure out a way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:52.320
spend those resources in a way that is supporting&nbsp;
what your deck is trying to do. This is actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:57.120
pretty similar to the process of learning a Real&nbsp;
Time Strategy game—becoming an average-skill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:01.120
Starcraft player basically just requires&nbsp;
two things: building up resource generation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:04.880
and then being able to spend all of the&nbsp;
resources you end up accumulating. We can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:09.200
see parallels to EDH battlecruiser decks with&nbsp;
this—as long as you’re completing the basic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:13.600
tasks like hitting land drops, playing some ramp&nbsp;
spells, and spending all of your mana each turn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:17.280
you’ll probably be having an okay time,&nbsp;
and once you’re at that point you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:21.440
turn your attention to the specifics of&nbsp;
what tasks your mana is accomplishing.

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:26.160
In the context of this type of deck, a deck&nbsp;
chiefly concerned with managing mana gained vs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:32.640
mana spent, all sorts of things can loosely fit&nbsp;
into the category of draw. Adding X-spells to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:37.280
deck will typically reduce its need for draw,&nbsp;
since they’re very effective at spending mana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:41.360
and the same goes for X-abilities and other&nbsp;
abilities that are useful to sink a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:46.960
mana into over a turn cycle. These aren’t exactly&nbsp;
draw, and you’d get some weird looks if you tried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:51.440
to call them that, but they absolutely perform&nbsp;
some of the same functions as draw in decks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:57.040
mostly focused on spending mana. The key here is&nbsp;
going to be making sure that mana is spent in ways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:01.920
that are actually effective at pushing toward&nbsp;
a deck’s goals. Throwing a random Fireball in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:07.520
an Isshin deck might make it better at spending&nbsp;
mana, but an X-cost damage spell probably isn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:12.880
a type of the card that will generally progress a&nbsp;
creature-based midrange deck. The best available&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:18.320
ways to spend mana will typically be creatures&nbsp;
with on-attack draw abilities that synergize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:22.480
with the deck’s commander, and there are also&nbsp;
a number of utility lands in Mardu colors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:26.920
that can soak up mana while accomplishing&nbsp;
useful tasks for a combat-centered deck.

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:31.760
Maybe you scoffed at the idea that a mana-sink&nbsp;
spell or ability could be considered alongside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:37.760
draw, and for a lot of decks, that’s an entirely&nbsp;
valid impulse. Some decks want an ongoing flow of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:43.200
actual factual cards they can play, whether that&nbsp;
be answers or utility creatures or anything else,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:48.640
and that brings me to a couple draw alternatives&nbsp;
that still lean in the direction of spending mana.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:52.640
The first is cards that exile cards and allow&nbsp;
you to play them for the next turn or two,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:57.920
with these effects sometimes being called “impulse&nbsp;
draw”. These don’t allow having cards stick around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:02.320
until a potential future point where they’re&nbsp;
useful, so they’re best utilized in decks with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:07.520
few situational cards and more general purpose&nbsp;
cards that the deck will rarely feel bad about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:13.920
casting. Another popular non-draw source of value&nbsp;
generation is recursion spells, cards that return&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:19.280
things from graveyard to hand. This could be&nbsp;
a big one-time option such as Seasons Past,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:24.400
and it could also be a recursion engine such as&nbsp;
Evolution Witness plus counter shenanigans, Scrap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:29.840
Trawler plus some artifacts, or even something&nbsp;
discrete like Phyrexian Reclamation. You could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:36.000
even include graveyard abilities such as flashback&nbsp;
or escape in this category. All of these options&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:42.800
serve a similar task—allowing already-played cards&nbsp;
to be reused again. These sorts of effects are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:47.760
typically going to be strongest in decks that&nbsp;
involve lots of redundancy and similar cards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:53.520
since returning a common card type from graveyard&nbsp;
to hand is going to be pretty similar in effect to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:59.600
drawing one of those cards. However, these still&nbsp;
tend to have limitations, in that they require&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:04.640
having stuff in the graveyard to function.&nbsp;
For a deck with lots of sacrifice or which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:09.200
based around instants and sorceries, key cards&nbsp;
will get into the graveyard all on their own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:14.080
but other types of decks might want sources of&nbsp;
self-mill or self-discard to keep their graveyard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:19.440
stocked with juicy options for returning.&nbsp;
Generally the more self-mill or wheel-style&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:24.400
effects you add to a deck, the more your recursion&nbsp;
spells will stray away from the task of spending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:31.280
mana and start to gain some genuine card selection&nbsp;
potential, and this brings me to the second goal.

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:36.640
The second goal of so-called draw is finding&nbsp;
particular cards you need, and this goal tends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:41.680
to be served by cards that might be considered&nbsp;
“filtering”. Cards that draw and discard, or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:45.920
otherwise filter through cards without obtaining&nbsp;
card advantage, are going to be better and better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:51.440
the more a deck cares about specific cards.&nbsp;
The most obvious case is in something like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:56.400
combo piece, but the same could go for a deck that&nbsp;
has five prominent centerpiece synergy cards that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:01.440
it would like to find one or more of. On a more&nbsp;
abstract level, filtering will have the greatest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:07.760
utility in decks with a high degree of variation&nbsp;
between cards, or variance between different power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:12.560
levels. Seeing three different variants of a&nbsp;
low-cost beater with a filtering spell like Dig&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:17.600
Through Time isn’t getting a whole lot of usage&nbsp;
out of the card, while seeing a bunch of wildly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:22.640
different cards is much more potent. Likewise,&nbsp;
if you’re casting Impulse, seeing four different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:27.680
cards of roughly the same power level is, on some&nbsp;
level, getting less use out of the card than if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:32.400
you instead saw three filler cards and a powerful&nbsp;
centerpiece. If we want to stretch the definition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:38.400
of filtering a little, and I certainly do, tutors&nbsp;
are the ultimate example of this—cards that search&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:43.840
a player’s library for a card that fits some set&nbsp;
of constraints. Solve the Equation is basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:49.680
a Silundi Vision that looks at unlimited cards&nbsp;
instead of 6, and Demonic Tutor is basically an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:55.840
Impulse that looks at unlimited cards instead of&nbsp;
4. Sorcery speed, though, so it’s pretty balanced.

00:06:55.840 --> 00:07:01.760
So, do tutors count as card draw? Do cards like&nbsp;
Impulse count as draw? It depends on what your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:07.600
deck’s goals are with running these cards. In some&nbsp;
decks it might be important to distinguish between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:13.520
what cards give card advantage as opposed to what&nbsp;
cards only give card selection. A spellslinger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:18.720
deck might run Impulse, and it might be a helpful&nbsp;
card for smoothing out gameplay, but if the deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:23.360
is currently running a fairly low number of&nbsp;
draw engines, it’s not necessarily going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:28.480
a helpful addition for purposes of maintaining&nbsp;
cards in hand. Likewise, if you have a really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:33.520
impactful card you’re tutoring for, a tutor very&nbsp;
well could feel like a draw spell, particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:38.720
if it’s a tutor that generally searches up some&nbsp;
sort of value engine. A Steelshaper’s Gift that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:42.960
exclusively finds Skullclamp followed by Sword&nbsp;
of Forge and Frontier? That’s a draw spell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:44.954
get it out of that tutor category. In other cases,&nbsp;
though, a tutor won’t make sense to think of as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.954 --> 00:07:49.680
draw. When I’m deckbuilding on Archidekt, I’ll&nbsp;
generally put tutors in my draw category in two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:55.280
cases: if they generally find a source of card&nbsp;
advantage, or if the function of tutors wholly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.280 --> 00:08:00.080
lines up with the function of drawing cards. For&nbsp;
example, if I’m playing a combo deck where the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:04.480
main thing my draw spells accomplish is digging&nbsp;
for combo pieces or tutors, then the purpose of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:10.400
my draw is almost exclusively card selection, and&nbsp;
tutors accomplish that. I’ve also reached the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:15.360
conclusion in the opposite direction—my Breena&nbsp;
deck is an aggro deck with lots of redundancy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:20.640
so tutors aren’t much better than basic card draw.&nbsp;
When I added Ayara’s Oathsworn because of its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:24.720
synergy with the commander, there was no reason&nbsp;
for me not to group it with the deck’s draw,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:29.760
because it’s performing the same task. However,&nbsp;
in other decks, tutors play a somewhat distinct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:35.040
role. In my strongest deck, I run tutors to find&nbsp;
Time Sieve because that’s what I need to win,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.040 --> 00:08:39.680
but I also run Bident of Thassa effects which&nbsp;
generically draw cards when my creatures hit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.680 --> 00:08:44.400
players. This can help find Time Sieve, but&nbsp;
it can also draw me into counterspells to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:49.360
protect my combo, and if Time Sieve is up&nbsp;
and running but I don’t have a full combo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:54.240
having an engine that gives me an extra 3-4&nbsp;
cards a turn is typically enough to turn a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:59.200
couple extra turns into a win by helping me&nbsp;
find things to give extra artifacts. Card&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:04.080
selection and card advantage are both helpful,&nbsp;
and having a mixture of selection-only cards and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:09.360
generic card draw that provides both works out to&nbsp;
fulfill the deck’s needs in the draw department.

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:14.960
We can actually construct a spectrum and place&nbsp;
all the types of cards I’ve mentioned today on it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.960 --> 00:09:21.040
On one side, we have pure selection—effects that&nbsp;
are all about seeing extra cards—and on the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.040 --> 00:09:26.560
side, pure advantage—cards that turn mana into&nbsp;
value without seeing any cards at all. Ledger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.560 --> 00:09:31.840
Shredder? Pure filtering. Tutors? Generally&nbsp;
also pure filtering, though a bit flexible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.840 --> 00:09:36.800
depending on what you play to get. Cards like&nbsp;
Dig Through Time still lean toward filtering,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:40.640
though at least here you’re getting some actual&nbsp;
factual card advantage as well by being one card&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:45.520
that finds two cards. I’d also put Greater&nbsp;
Good here, a powerful green draw enchantment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.520 --> 00:09:50.240
that tends to churn through cards at a fast&nbsp;
rate. Nearing the center of this spectrum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:54.800
you’ve got things that generate a decent bit of&nbsp;
card advantage but also do a bit of extra digging,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.800 --> 00:09:59.680
and this includes my personal favorite draw-X&nbsp;
card in most decks, Pull from Tomorrow. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.680 --> 00:10:03.280
instant speed, reasonably costed, and&nbsp;
the disadvantage of discarding a card&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.280 --> 00:10:07.920
is typically negligible since it’s attached&nbsp;
to a card that draws a bunch of cards first.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:12.880
You could also put something like Necropotence&nbsp;
here, since absent a Reliquary Tower you’ll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:17.040
generally be exiling some amount of what you&nbsp;
draw even if there’s a lot of volume of cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:22.160
entering your hand. Generic card draw leans a bit&nbsp;
toward advantage as compared to other options,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:26.400
though a lot of the strongest draw is just generic&nbsp;
card draw, so oftentimes decks will focus on this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:31.520
even if they mostly care about card selection.&nbsp;
After that you’ve got impulse draw and recursion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:36.320
engines, and while the former is relatively&nbsp;
straightforward, the latter will vary a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:41.520
depending on how much it’s supported. Recursion&nbsp;
spells might lean very close to advantage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:45.200
though they also might have some degree of&nbsp;
selection as well depending on how much self-mill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:50.560
and discard a deck has. It’s often far easier&nbsp;
to get cards into the graveyard than into hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:55.360
but the tools for hating on graveyards are far&nbsp;
stronger and far more ubiquitous than cards that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.360 --> 00:11:01.920
force opponents to discard are, so recursion has&nbsp;
some natural limits in how reliable it can be. On&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.920 --> 00:11:06.840
the far end of things we have abilities that can&nbsp;
soak up an unlimited amount of mana. Kessig Wolf&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.840 --> 00:11:11.280
Run is a land with a powerful activated ability&nbsp;
that a deck can sink as much mana as they like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.280 --> 00:11:17.200
into. It will never find you any cards, but it can&nbsp;
grant the value of giving a creature you control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.200 --> 00:11:22.640
+X/+0 and trample, and the only cost to have this&nbsp;
ability available is running a colorless land.

00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:27.440
So this is a spectrum of what different styles&nbsp;
of draw and filtering will accomplish in terms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.440 --> 00:11:32.320
of value and card selection, but I worry that&nbsp;
all this categorization and visual flourish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:36.800
and such like might serve to abstract the&nbsp;
issue beyond what’s helpful. So, how do you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.800 --> 00:11:42.240
find the best options for your deck, and how do&nbsp;
you know when you have enough? Let’s start with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.240 --> 00:11:47.120
that first question. Broadly, what types of value&nbsp;
generation and filtering you’ll want will depend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.120 --> 00:11:52.320
on your gameplan, your colors, and what sorts&nbsp;
of stuff your deck is made up of. Generally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:56.800
the more variation that exists between your&nbsp;
different cards, the more you’ll want to focus on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.800 --> 00:12:01.840
filtering. If you have lots of situational cards,&nbsp;
or a handful of specific cards you want to find,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.840 --> 00:12:06.560
you’ll want to lean toward cards that allow&nbsp;
you to see more of your deck. Look for options&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.560 --> 00:12:10.400
closer to the right end of the spectrum&nbsp;
I gave earlier—tutors, draw and discard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.400 --> 00:12:15.760
topdeck manipulating cards, and so on. Notably,&nbsp;
it’s generally pretty rare that a deck will want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.760 --> 00:12:21.120
exclusively card selection, so your task will&nbsp;
be in figuring out what amount and what forms of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.120 --> 00:12:26.480
actual draw you want to pair with your filtering.&nbsp;
In some cases, selection and advantage options&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.480 --> 00:12:31.360
will mesh together quite nicely—recursion and&nbsp;
abilities that can be used from the graveyard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.360 --> 00:12:35.600
work great as an advantage source to pair&nbsp;
with card selection in the form of draw and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.600 --> 00:12:41.040
discard—the selection feeds advantage options that&nbsp;
might be a bit mediocre on their own. Likewise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:45.120
running tutors to find a centerpiece draw&nbsp;
engine is another popular way to combine a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.120 --> 00:12:50.080
selection option with an advantage option. For a&nbsp;
third, somewhat more specific example, sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.080 --> 00:12:54.720
you’ll have creatures that do some sort of topdeck&nbsp;
filtering on an attack or combat damage trigger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.720 --> 00:13:00.720
and pairing these with Curiosity-style effects&nbsp;
can set you up quite well for a second main phase.

00:13:00.720 --> 00:13:05.040
On the opposite end of things, decks that lean&nbsp;
toward advantage as the preferred card type&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.040 --> 00:13:10.160
typically include more redundancy—put simply,&nbsp;
you don’t need to spend nearly as much effort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.160 --> 00:13:14.560
finding a specific sort of effect if you’re&nbsp;
running 10 or 15 of it. I’ve also found that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.560 --> 00:13:19.680
needing less card selection tends to correlate&nbsp;
with a couple other things as well. For one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.680 --> 00:13:24.640
decks that rely on and leverage their commander&nbsp;
more aggressively tend to run less card selection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.640 --> 00:13:28.960
since they already have their most&nbsp;
important card available. Secondly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.960 --> 00:13:33.680
decks that ramp more tend to run less card&nbsp;
selection and more actual factual draw,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.680 --> 00:13:38.160
since they can generally afford to sink mana into&nbsp;
multiple cards rather than digging around for one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.160 --> 00:13:43.520
ideal one. Neither of these are set in stone&nbsp;
rules—you’ll have decks that really want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.520 --> 00:13:47.760
find specific supporting cards for their powerful&nbsp;
commander, and you’ll also have decks with very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.760 --> 00:13:52.240
specific ideas of what they want to do once&nbsp;
they’ve ramped up to a lot of mana. These are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.240 --> 00:13:56.720
just trends to keep in mind when you’re trying&nbsp;
to identify your deck’s needs. With that, let’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.720 --> 00:14:02.160
talk about the final question of the video—how&nbsp;
much advantage acquisition should your deck run?

00:14:02.160 --> 00:14:05.920
If you’re playing a deck that doesn’t care&nbsp;
as much about selection, focus on ability to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.920 --> 00:14:11.280
generate mana versus ability to spend mana. If&nbsp;
you’re missing land drops, add lands. If you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.280 --> 00:14:16.320
falling behind in gameplay volume, add ramp.&nbsp;
If you’re finding yourself with excess mana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:21.520
add some mana sinks like draw, abilities, or&nbsp;
graveyard themes. If you’re finding yourself with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.520 --> 00:14:26.480
a lot of cards in hand, try trimming a bit of draw&nbsp;
or orienting your deck more toward mana-efficient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.480 --> 00:14:31.920
but card-inefficient options like single target&nbsp;
removal, low-cost ramp, and situational parity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.920 --> 00:14:36.480
breakers. You might think that your first priority&nbsp;
should be the gameplan of your deck, and that does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.480 --> 00:14:41.360
matter for sure, but for just about every type of&nbsp;
effect you could want to lean toward there will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.360 --> 00:14:45.920
versions that are more mana efficient and versions&nbsp;
that are more card efficient, so you can always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.920 --> 00:14:52.240
be keeping an eye on that balance. Now let’s say&nbsp;
you care more about card selection. For starters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.240 --> 00:14:57.360
you’ll want to make sure whatever you’re running&nbsp;
card selection for is being attained—are you able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.360 --> 00:15:02.000
to filter into key cards? Are you able to find&nbsp;
your situational answers in reasonable lengths&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:06.880
of time? Once this seems to be in a decent&nbsp;
spot, start keeping an eye on how often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.880 --> 00:15:12.400
you’re able to find your actual factual draw.&nbsp;
Typically actual draw will be, at a minimum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.400 --> 00:15:17.200
helpful for finding interaction for what opponents&nbsp;
are doing, particularly if you’re focused on mana&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.200 --> 00:15:19.440
efficient but card inefficient options like&nbsp;
single target removal or counterspells. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.440 --> 00:15:22.160
also might be key for getting&nbsp;
more definitively ahead once you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.160 --> 00:15:25.280
have some amount of setup complete but&nbsp;
don’t yet have control over the game.

00:15:26.240 --> 00:15:29.920
As you’ve probably noticed, the point of this&nbsp;
video hasn’t been so much to tell you what is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.920 --> 00:15:33.760
or isn’t draw, but rather to give you a&nbsp;
framework to work with in analyzing cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.760 --> 00:15:38.560
and deciding if they’re what your deck needs. A&nbsp;
lot of times building commander decks is reduced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.560 --> 00:15:43.680
to following a template, and if a template calls&nbsp;
for X number of draw spells, you’re gonna wanna&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.680 --> 00:15:48.720
know what counts as draw. And, don’t get me wrong,&nbsp;
that sort of approach is a perfectly fine way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.720 --> 00:15:58.480
toss together a deck quickly. But take a bit of&nbsp;
extra time to figure out how all the individual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.480 --> 00:16:03.360
working parts of your deck fit together,&nbsp;
and you’ll have a much stronger end product.

00:16:03.360 --> 00:16:07.280
